## CORE METHODOLOGY

### Phase 1: Data Understanding & Preparation

> **DATA-FIRST LAW:** never propose or draw a visualization for data that was
> not **queried and validated**. If the dashboard is fed by a queryable
> source (SQL database via an MCP tool, an API, a CSV export), discover the
> real shape BEFORE building: inspect the schema/columns for the target
> tables, profile distinct values / ranges / null-rates on the columns you
> will visualize, and resolve any join relationships via whatever knowledge
> the project exposes (schema docs, `COMMENT ON COLUMN`, an ERD). Then
> validate each headline metric against a raw query. A chart whose number
> you cannot reproduce at the source does not ship.

Before writing any code, you MUST:

1. **Analyze the Data Structure**
   - Identify all dimensions (categorical variables) and measures (numerical variables)
   - Determine data granularity and aggregation levels
   - Map relationships between entities

2. **Define Key Metrics**
   - Primary KPIs (3-8 headline numbers, typically 4-6 for most dashboards)
   - Secondary metrics (supporting context)
   - Derived calculations (percentages, differences, rates, scores)

3. **Plan Segmentation Strategy**
   - Identify natural groupings in the data
   - Determine which dimensions warrant separate tabs
   - Plan cross-tabulation combinations (e.g., "Dimension A × Dimension B")

4. **Data Completeness Check**
   Always ensure you have:
   - **Absolute values** (raw counts, totals)
   - **Relative values** (percentages, proportions)
   - **Comparative values** (differences, deltas, period-over-period)
   - **Classification breakdowns** (categories within categories)

### Phase 2: Information Architecture

Structure your dashboard following this hierarchy:

```
DASHBOARD
├── Header (branding, title, context badges, date)
├── KPI Strip (4-6 primary metrics)
├── Summary Cards (if applicable, e.g., category totals, classification summaries)
├── Tab Navigation (one tab per major dimension)
│   └── Each Tab Contains:
│       ├── Chart Grid (1-3 columns based on content)
│       │   ├── Primary visualization (distribution, comparison, or trend)
│       │   └── Secondary visualization (breakdown, classification, or detail)
│       │   └── [Optional] Tertiary visualization for complex data
│       └── Detailed Data Table (full width)
└── Insights Tab (findings + recommendations)
```

### Phase 3: Visual Design System

> **Tokens, not fixed values.** The system below is a generic STARTING
> POINT (dark theme, AA contrast) defined as CSS custom properties —
> swap the values for YOUR brand's palette/typography; the structure (named
> tokens, never hex scattered across the CSS) is what matters and does not change.

```css
:root {
  /* backgrounds — dark theme, 4 elevation levels */
  --bg-body: #0F172A;
  --bg-secondary: #1E293B;
  --bg-card: #1E293B;
  --bg-elevated: #334155;
  --hover: #2A3548;
  --border: #334155;

  /* text */
  --text-primary: #E5E7EB;
  --text-secondary: #9CA3AF;

  /* REPLACE with your brand's accent — this is just a neutral placeholder */
  --accent: #3B82F6;
  --accent-alpha-20: rgba(59, 130, 246, 0.2);

  /* traffic light (universal — keep these roles even when swapping the tones) */
  --success: #22C55E;
  --danger: #EF4444;
  --warning: #EAB308;
  --info: #3B82F6;

  /* typography — REPLACE with your brand's font; mono is for
     tabular alignment of numbers (JetBrains Mono, Roboto Mono, etc.) */
  --font-base: system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
  --font-mono: ui-monospace, "SFMono-Regular", monospace;
}

body {
  background: var(--bg-body);
  color: var(--text-primary);
  font-family: var(--font-base);
  font-size: 13.5px;
  color-scheme: dark;
}
.kpi-value { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 24px; font-weight: 700; }
table.dt td.num { font-family: var(--font-mono); text-align: right; }
```

`Chart.defaults.color`, axis borders and font must point to the same
tokens (`var(--text-secondary)`, `var(--border)`, `var(--font-base)`) — never
hardcode a hex outside this `:root` block.

**pt-BR / localization:** if the audience is BR, format numbers with `,` decimal
and the `bi`/`mi`/`mil` ladder for currency (`fmtBRL`); `'—'` for null. If the
audience is another locale, adapt the formatting function — the PRINCIPLE
(magnitude ladder + explicit null placeholder, never `NaN`/empty) is what
is preserved.

