## DATA PRESENTATION RULES

### Rule 1: Always Show Both Absolute and Relative

❌ Wrong: "40.6% of non-respondents are new users"
✅ Right: "1,969 users (40.6%) are new users who didn't respond"

### Rule 2: Include All Classification Levels

If data has categories (e.g., Status: Active/Churned, Risk: High/Medium/Low), always show:
- Count per category
- Percentage per category
- Visual distribution (mini-bar)

### Rule 3: Calculate and Display Differences

When comparing groups, always show:
- Raw difference (absolute)
- Percentage point difference (p.p.)
- Direction indicator (positive/negative badge)

### Rule 4: Highlight Problem Areas

In `table.dt`, color the offending **cell** with the semaphore classes —
`td.bad` (danger), `td.warn` (warning), `td.ok` (success) — for:
- Highest negative values / outliers requiring attention
- Threshold breaches (apply the same semaphore logic the charts use)

No fixed zebra striping and no persistent `highlight-row` class — row
emphasis is only `tr:hover td{background:var(--hover)}`.

### Rule 5: Provide Context in Tables

Every `table.dt` should have:
- Sortable, clickable `th` (cursor pointer, hover state); abbreviated headers if needed
- Numeric columns right-aligned with `class="num"`, monospace font (`var(--font-mono)`)
- Cell semaphore (`.ok/.warn/.bad`) and inline pills/badges for quick scanning

